{"reference":"Job 11","book":"Job","chapter":11,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Larger Debate","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Job 11 is part of the extended debate that follows seven days of shared silence. Job has lamented his birth, and Zophar now joins Eliphaz and Bildad in treating Job’s suffering as evidence of wrongdoing—a diagnosis Job rejects. The book’s later rebuke of the friends means that Zophar’s confident accusations and promises should not be read as unqualified guidance for responding to another person’s suffering. The chapter’s call to become “steadfast” also should not turn Job’s endurance into a demand for silent submission to abuse; in the larger story, endurance can describe persistence through suffering and the outcome that follows, without ruling out escape or protection.","confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_011_56f14b666495e50da3afb6d1","reader_path_job_011_d01aab6fd90947cad48ccfc1"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}